This document demonstrates bridge mode, where English prose sits next to structured CSLv3 notation. Readers unfamiliar with the notation can still follow the intent while the parser extracts only the structured regions for static analysis.

The goal of the document is to describe an HTTP request handler pipeline. The prose portions describe the what and why, while the structured blocks give the machine-readable signatures.

The request type has a method field that holds the HTTP verb, a path field that holds the URL path portion, and a body field that holds the raw payload bytes. The response type has a status field that holds a numeric HTTP status and a body field that holds the response payload bytes. Both types are defined in CSLv3 records.

The function's contract is that any valid request maps to exactly one response. There is no path where the handler returns zero responses or more than one response.

The handler takes a reference to a request and returns a response. It is the single entry point, and all routing, processing, and output generation happens inside it.

The determinism invariant states that the same request should yield the same response. The request-to-response mapping is total and single-valued.
